                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Compares well to games like Antichamber and Portal, of course, but to my liking also achieves the suspension of the real even further as with a game like Proteus. I’d even like to go ahead and introduce a term to describe this particular genre: Waking Simulators.\n\nSimple mechanics, smooth physics and a sandbox of puzzles to direct your attention in a surreal dreamscape that challenges the possibilities of the real world. The sound and visual design is mature and professional and follows along the trend set by other impressive audio/visual design in games like Stanley Parable, The Talos Principle, Awkward Dimensions Redux and The Witness, begging the question of whether good developers choose first person adventure puzzlers or if the nature of contemplative exploration and problem solving gets to a deeper core of human experience than most gaming. Perhaps games are the most expressive form of art available, but at the same time perhaps the ones that are well executed simply manage to hide the boundaries of an exploration of art within our consciousness that is mutual to media and space.\n\nThis is still Minecraft gameplay without the inventory, vulnerability and monsters. Puzzles will still offer a relative challenge or frustration depending upon your experience with these controls and mechanics, and are sometimes predictable if you've played some of the stuff I've already mentioned, but it does strive for novelty and exhibits unique settings worth seeing should anyone read this review when cost isn't so much of a concern. (I'm getting that $100 Panoramical game after I'm done bankrupting myself on gardening another year, so don't ask me about value.) You build stacks of blocks that can be tipped over, sliced into segments with their own weight and momentum, or wiped away like an etch-a-sketch in a perfectly controlled explosion of negative light. It’s an open world, so you don’t need to go anywhere and can just stay where you are, but the variety of striking aesthetics will pull you along and you may even become compelled by the passing narrative. Which is first presented in a short film sequence that is actually quite superb in its visceral interpretation. The voice acting isn’t even annoying, which should count as excellent by a video game measure.\n\nHonestly, if you’re bored by video games as I often am you won’t find a lasting attachment to this one, but what I’m trying to say is that perhaps a game like Bokida might embody that boredom in a mutually transcendent space. The search for meaning, thrills or bliss doesn’t particularly change from one mode of being to the next (redirecting one’s attention from the world to the game world), mostly because WASD is so mechanical, but this one manages to achieve the expected flow of a modern day FPS and provides mechanics that function well enough not to be a distraction from where the game really shines: its space.\n\nI haven’t played in VR and don’t know how much better the experience would be anyway, but the game does excel best at audio and visual presentation so it’s to be expected that it might improve the game even further, especially if you could simply chop your hand rather than having to rotate the angle of your cut with a mouse and keyboard, note to dev should they read this,",
